WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:06.560
The Riddler always pushes Batman to his mental
limits, and it's no different in "The Batman."

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.740
Batman's foe leaves cards for him to find,
but there is more to them than initially meets

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.740
the eye.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.370
Keep watching to learn why the Riddler's cards
mean more than you think.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:20.550
The Riddler has never been more of an enigmatic
figure than in "The Batman."

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:25.130
While previous live-action iterations have
seen the character as a bit of a jokester,

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.430
particularly when it comes to Jim Carrey's
take on the character in "Batman Forever,"

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.500
he's deadly serious in this one, taking on
the aesthetic of a zodiac-type serial killer

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.699
in Gotham.

00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:40.489
To make that real-world connection all the
more apparent, Riddler leaves behind puzzles

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:45.899
and ciphers at the scenes of his crimes for
Batman and Gotham P.D. to solve.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:48.769
He's cryptic, all the way down to his capture.

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:51.870
That's the point where the cops realize he
has two separate I.D.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.230
cards on him.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:58.480
However, Riddler may be even slyer than most
viewers initially gave him credit for.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.899
As it turns out, he hid secret Batman references
throughout many of the film's scenes in plain

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:04.899
sight.

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:08.220
But you have to be a real Batman aficionado
to catch all of them.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.520
It becomes a recurring motif throughout "The
Batman" that Riddler leaves a card behind

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.550
for the titular vigilante at each crime scene.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.570
The cards hold significance to the plot by
typically containing a riddle for Batman to

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.570
solve.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.360
"Haven't a clue.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.170
Let's play a game, just me and you."

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.200
But the cards themselves make for fun Easter
eggs all on their own for eagle-eyed fans.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.040
Each card connects to a different Batman villain.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.210
Perhaps the best-known example of this is
with the first card containing an owl featured

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.680
in "The Batman" trailer.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.560
Many fans quickly pointed out the connection
to the Court of Owls storyline, with many

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.840
believing the arc could factor into the sequel's
plot.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.040
It's unknown if that will be the case, but
it nonetheless makes for a fun addition.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.220
Similar Easter eggs can be found with the
remaining four cards, as each one connects

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.070
with a different Batman villain.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.820
The second card contains an image of a mad
scientist who bears a striking resemblance

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.540
to Hugo Strange.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.909
The third card features a picture of a woman
dressed in green, which immediately makes

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:10.640
one think of Poison Ivy.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.780
The fourth card is where things get tricky.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.530
It's just a black background with two large
eyes looking toward the viewer.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.640
It's reminiscent of the Cheshire Cat in "Alice
in Wonderland," and of course, Batman has

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.879
a villain who goes by the name of Mad Hatter.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:30.170
Last but not least, the fifth and final card
has a boy with a puppet.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:35.489
Fans of "Batman: The Animated Series" will
likely recall The Ventriloquist and Scarface,

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:39.150
a man and puppet duo who commit various crimes
in Gotham.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:43.749
Any of these villains would make fun additions
to "The Batman" mythos, but for now, they're

00:02:43.749 --> 00:02:45.379
relegated to references.

00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:50.019
Batman has some of the most iconic adversaries
out of any villain out there.

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:54.480
No doubt this is helped in part from the litany
of Batman projects that have materialized

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.909
over the years, giving fans a glimpse into
the likes of Scarecrow, Penguin, and more.

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:04.209
While the cards hinting at other villains
within Batman lore would be enough for most

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:09.349
filmmakers, there are additional, stronger
hints that some of Batman's foes are out there,

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:13.730
just waiting to make a grand entrance in "The
Batman 2" and beyond.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.090
The most obvious reference to this is when
Riddler is locked away toward the end of the

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.090
film.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:22.969
He's naturally bummed that his plans went
into the sewer, and he could use some cheering

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:23.969
up.

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:26.290
That's where his cellmate next door comes
in handy.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:31.139
While this individual never receives a name,
anyone who's seen a Batman property before

00:03:31.139 --> 00:03:36.389
can surmise it's Joker by his signature laugh
and reference to clowns.

00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:39.799
"You can call me Joker."

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:42.760
That's not the only potential reference found
in the film.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.980
As Batman fights Riddler's minions, he finds
himself between a rock and a hard place.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.959
He's beaten and worn down, but it's Catwoman
who is at the mercy of one of their foes.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:57.790
To give himself a quick burst of energy, Batman
reaches for a vial containing a green liquid.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.109
He then injects himself with the material,
and suddenly, he's on his feet once again

00:04:02.109 --> 00:04:03.779
and beating the bad guys.

00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:08.590
Given the enhanced strength and brutality
he displayed, it seems the substance is awfully

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:13.599
similar to the Venom Bane has used throughout
his run as one of Batman's villains.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:18.259
Could this mean we'll get Joker and Bane in
one movie in the future?

00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:19.720
One can only hope.

